The ambient (libbitrt) collector DOES fire under `BIT_GC=stress` — this was
wrong before #4160. Measured over 8 runs: default-policy runs report
`collections=0` every time; `BIT_GC=stress` runs report ~141,640-141,800,
never zero or anywhere near it. Most of that volume is inside
`phaseCacheGuard`'s and `phaseFreeListGuard`'s own large allocation loops
(RC_SRC_OBJS / RC_FAT_OBJS = 12,000 objects each) — real safepoints on every
back-edge once `BIT_GC=stress` is set. Default and `BIT_GC=stress` runs still
produce byte-identical stdout, confirmed the same 8 runs.

This program is still exempt from the harness's stress/default collection-
count proof, but not because the ambient collector never runs or "stays
under the trigger" — it does not, by four orders of magnitude. It is exempt
because every quantity the three private heaps (the top-level one and the
two #4122 phases) assert on — spanCount, spansOk/mismatches,
popcount(bitmap) vs spanTotal-spanFree, and the recycling guards'
checked/mismatch/violation/leaked counts — is read out of that heap's own
PRIVATE `gcBindMemory`-bound state, never out of anything the ambient
collector scans or sweeps. Whether the ambient collector ran 0 times or
141,800 times cannot move any of those numbers.

The one thing an ambient collection COULD break — each heap's backing arrays
being swept once `gcBindMemory` has taken their raw addresses — is checked
directly, not by the generic collection-count proof: the top-level heap's
`rooted` line reads `len(arena)`/`len(indexArena)`/`len(roots)` after
teardown, and the recycling phases read every result field before their own
private `gcDeinit`. The header's #4097 note records that this program has run
140k+ ambient collections with no corruption while flagging that as an
observation of this file's current codegen, not a guarantee — re-measure
before trusting it if this file changes. That is the actual rooting property
under test here, not the collection count.
